<div id="outline-container-orgf536cd9" class="outline-2">
<h2 id="orgf536cd9">Keeping the team on schedule</h2>
<div id="text-orgf536cd9" class="outline-text-2">
<p>If you are coordinating with people who don’t use Emacs, which is almost everyone, the workflow is:</p>
<ol class="org-ol">
<li>You maintain the org file as the planning document.</li>
<li>You export to HTML (or PDF, or a quick PNG) and share it to a central location.</li>
<li>Updates come back as comments or from a meeting; you transcribe them into the org file.</li>
</ol>
<p>It may sound inefficient, but in practice it’s the same number of steps as maintaining a separate Asana board, minus the SaaS subscription, plus the fact that your project plan now lives next to your notes and your agenda, and you’re not locked into a proprietary format.</p>
